Here is a simple program to find the common elements in two arrays without using any extra data structures.

```
#include<stdio.h>

int main() {
    int first_array[5] = {1, 2, 3, 4, 5};
    int second_array[5] = {2, 5, 6, 7, 8};
    int i, j;
    int common_elements = 0;

    for (i = 0; i < 5; i++) {
        for (j = 0; j < 5; j++) {
            if (first_array[i] == second_array[j]) {
                common_elements++;
            }
        }
    }

    printf("The common elements are %d", common_elements);
    return 0;
}
```

This program uses a for-loop to iterate through each element of both the arrays. In each iteration of the for loop, we check if the element of the first array is equal to the element of the second array. If it is, we increment the count of common elements. After the for loop is finished, we print the total count of common elements.

In this case, the common elements are 2 because the first two elements of the first array are equal to the second two elements of the second array.</s>

