    int first_array[] = {1, 2, 3, 4, 5};
    int second_array[] = {2, 5, 6, 7, 8};
    int i, j;
    int common_elements = 0;
    std::map<int, int> first_hash_map, second_hash_map;

    for (i = 0; i < 5; i++) {
        first_hash_map[first_array[i]] = 1;
        second_hash_map[second_array[i]] = 1;
    }

    for (i = 0; i < 5; i++) {
        for (j = 0; j < 5; j++) {
            if (first_hash_map[first_array[i]] == second_hash_map[second_array[j]]) {
                common_elements++;
            }
        }
    }

    printf("The common elements are %d", common_elements);
    return 0;
}
```

In this example, we use a hashmap `first_hash_map` and `second_hash_map` to store the elements of the first and second arrays respectively. We iterate over the elements of the arrays and add the elements to the hashmap. For each element of the array, we check if it is already present in the hashmap. If it is, we increment the count of common elements. The for loop iterates over the elements of the array, and the for loop iterates over all the elements of the hashmap. This way, we can find the common elements in both the arrays in O(n) time.</s>

